{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Relational Data Model in DBMS "}, {"type": "position", "content": 10}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.3"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when talking about relational data model in DBMS"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Exchange written and spoken information on principles, concepts, data base objects related to relational database management system in spoken or written texts.   Describe relational database, its concepts, and principles in spoken or written texts. "}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses information a wider range of contexts about exchanging technical information on software programming and software solutions, using appropriate grammar and vocabulary at this level. Recognizes simple ideas on concepts on software programming, software solutions and database management."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob10re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Simple Present Passive Interrogative"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<strong>Simple present passive interrogative</strong> is mainly used to give more importance to the action instead to the person performing it. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Simple present passive interrogative sometimes is used because it is more polite. </br></br>  The structure for simple present passive interrogative is: </br></br>  (WH question) Am/ Is / Are + object + verb past participle</br></br>Example:</br><i> How many concepts <b>are included</b> in the relational data model?</i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Are the relations saved as tables?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "What are the requirements needed for a relation to be valid?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "One last thing, can the relational model be taken as the primary data model?"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Comparisons"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "There are certain structures used to make <strong>comparisons</strong> in English two of them as: as\u2026 as and not as\u2026 as. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Some  of the most used comparative connector are <strong>not as\u2026 as </strong>  and <strong>as\u2026 as</strong></br> The comparative <strong>not as\u2026 as</strong>  is used to show that something is superior compared to another thing using a negative structure. </br></br>  let's check its structure and examples: </br> one object+ verb to be + not as + adjective + as + second object </br></br>Example:</br><i>Tables are not <b>as easy to understand as</b> tuple. </i></br></br>  The comparative as\u2026 as is used to put to things at the same level, to say they are equal. </br> let's check its structure and examples: </br></br> one object+ verb to be +  as + adjective + as + second object </br></br>Example:</br><i>The relation instance is <b>as difficult as</b> the relation schema. </i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "That is not as easy to explain as you might think."}, {"type": "example", "content": "The exam is as difficult as the last one."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Well, as long as it works for the specific purposes of a project, yes, sir!"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "process "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a series of actions leading to an objective"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: It is the main data model used by almost everybody to store and process data."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob10aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "relations "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a connection or association between two or more parts"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Tables are the ones that store all the relations between entities in form of charts with rows and columns."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob10aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "tuple "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a row of values in a relational database"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The Tuple is one row of a table and it has only one record."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob10aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "table "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "structure used to organize information"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Then, there is the relation instance, which is a determined number of tuples."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob10aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "constraints "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a limitation or restriction"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: There are some integrity relational constraints that need to be accomplished."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob10aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "integrity "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "something that is complete that is a whole"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: There are some integrity relational constraints that need to be accomplished."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob10aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Write a short summary about Relational Data Model. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create a post with the main concepts of Relational Data Model."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share with your partners the post you created."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I write a short summary about Relational Data Model. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I create a post with the main concepts of Relational Data Model. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share with your partners the post I created."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe7n7le02ob10re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong>Teacher:</strong> Ok Mark, today I will test your knowledge; I will make some questions. The answers are very simple. The exam is as difficult as the last one. <br/>\n<strong>Mark:</strong> Understood, sir. <br/>\n<strong>Teacher:</strong> First, tell me about the relational data model.<br/>\n<strong>Mark:</strong> It is the main data model used by almost everybody to store and process data. It is used often because it provides storage efficiency. <br/>\n<strong>Teacher:</strong> Can you develop a little bit more on that? Are the relations saved as tables?<br/>\n<strong>Mark:</strong>  Yes sir. There are six concepts. <br/></div>"}, {"time": "40500", "image": "enpe7n7le02ob10re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<div align=\"justify\">Tables are the ones that store all the relations between entities in form of charts with rows and columns. The Tuple is one row of a table and it has only one record. Then, there is the relation instance, which is a determined number of tuples; the relation schema that presents the associations between the table names, attributes and the attribute's names.  The relation key is the attributes that identify a specific row exclusively. Finally, the attribute domain, that is the pre-assigned importance of an attribute.<br/>\n<strong>Teacher:</strong> Excellent Mark! Now what are the requirements needed for a relation to be valid?</div>"}, {"time": "92000", "image": "enpe7n7le02ob10re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong>Mark</strong>: That is not as easy to explain as you might think. For a relation to be valid, there are some integrity relational constraints that need to be accomplished. There are three kinds: the key constrains, that is the slight subset of attributes that make a tuple unique; the domain constrains, which means that every attribute has to have a detailed set of values that cannot be unreal; and the referential integrity constrains, that refers to the connection that can exist between two key attributes in different relations.</br>\n<strong>Teacher</strong>: Good job, Mark! One last thing, can the relational model be taken as the primary data model?</br>\n<strong>Mark</strong>: Well, as long as it works for the specific purposes of a project, yes, sir!</div>"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n7le02ob10re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n7le02ob10re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can recall basic information about the relational data model in DBMS.  "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can discuss basic concepts in the relational data model.  "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about constraints in the relational data model.  "}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "How do you think a database can be related to a different one?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What types of relationships are there among databases?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedb